      "text_raw": "One of the three cells of the\nprevious amplifier is shown\nin this slide.\nThe voltage gain A only\nv\ndepends on the transcon-\nductances. Depending on\nthe DC biasing currents and\nthe widths, a small gain of\n5–8 is easily achieved. The\ntotal gain for three similar\nstages is therefore, easily\nover 100.\nFor the calculation of this\ngain, the current I\nDS2\nthrough transistor M2 is\nassumed to be constant. It\nis divided over the transistors M1 and M2 by factor l. Since all lengths are taken the same, this\nfactor l also determines the ratio of the widths W to W .\n3 1\nThe gain A is now readily calculated. It is shown next.\nv",
      "text": "One of the three cells of the previous amplifier is shown in this slide. The voltage gain A only v depends on the transconductances. Depending on the DC biasing currents and the widths, a small gain of 5–8 is easily achieved. The total gain for three similar stages is therefore, easily over 100. For the calculation of this gain, the current I DS2 through transistor M2 is assumed to be constant. It is divided over the transistors M1 and M2 by factor l. Since all lengths are taken the same, this factor l also determines the ratio of the widths W to W . 3 1 The gain A is now readily calculated. It is shown next. v"
    }
